1395/selection/0a4eebc40ee4300ad9592ac0e72aec84514efdaed3c1f72c6e8ac211c2002024"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341395" :question/id #uuid "17cd3e1b-aa72-56f6-b60e-28a9b8497519" :question/math-academy-id "q-341395" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $L{{f}^{″}(t)}$ for $s>1,$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ ${f}^{′}(0)=-1,$ $f(0)=5,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{5s+4}{(s-1)(s+2)},\\,s>1.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}$ piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\nL{{f}^{″}(t)}={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{″}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5s+4}{(s-1)(s+2)}-s\\cdot 5-(-1) \\\\  & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5s+4}{(s-1)(s+2)}-(5s-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}(5s+4)-(5s-1)(s-1)(s+2)}{(s-1)(s+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{s}^{3}+4{s}^{2}-(5{s}^{3}+4{s}^{2}-11s+2)}{(s-1)(s+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{11s-2}{(s-1)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-341395/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e59d43c-f723-521e-b7cf-6767195483c2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341395"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
